   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="FR">Output 1.2. Increased institutional capacity of workers&#x2019; organizations</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="FR">Between 2019 and May 2021, the two main trade union confederations, Conf&#xE9;d&#xE9;ration des Syndicats du Burundi (COSYBU) and Conf&#xE9;d&#xE9;ration Syndicale du Burundi (CSB), have increased their membership densities by recruiting new members, mainly from the informal economy. The membership of COSYBU and CSB increased from 186,004 and 14,261 in December 2019 to 264,455 including 54,112 females (i.e. 70.33 per cent overall growth and 20.46 per cent growth in female membership) and 39,396 including 3,850 females (i.e. 27 per cent overall growth and 10 per cent growth in female membership) in May 2021, respectively.

The main innovative organizing strategies adopted included power mapping, networking, campaigning, lobbying and communication with key stakeholders. These approaches were guided by the &apos;Fish Tank Model&apos; comprising a quiet phase that involved initiating contacts, recruiting opinion leaders, setting up and training organizing committees, and an open phase. This entailed newly recruited members signing member registration cards, organizing union elections, identifying and implementing actions to address workers&#x2019; needs, and forging strategic alliances with pro-worker civil society organizations with the aim of pursuing a common course of action. The newly unionized members now enjoy a certain degree of labour protection, including social dialogue and collective bargaining coverage. In July 2021, the ILO contracted an external consultant that supported the two confederation to undertake a study on Needs Assessment and the Mapping of Trade Union Membership in Burundi.

Since February 2020, the ILO provided technical support to the confedrations with the aim of identifying key messages that informed their campaigns for workers&#x2019; safe return to work following the authorities lifting of the COVID-19 pandemic lockdown. 

In June 2021, the ILO further developed an &quot;organizing resource manual&quot; to guide the unions (and affiliate organizations) in their campaigns for recruiting new members.</narrative>
   </description>
