   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="FR">Output 7.2. Increased capacity of member States to ensure safe and healthy working conditions</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="FR">During the second quarter of 2020, the government and social partners adopted and published in the Official Gazette the Labour Code with OSH provisions, including duties for employers and workers in promoting safe work place through hazard identification, management and control (Title V, chapters 1 and 2); organization of occupational medicine at company level (Title V, chapters 3); basic requirement of building serving as work places (Title V, chapters4), establishment, composition and OSH function of national tripartite labour committee; general provisions on all working equipment utilized by companies  (Title V, chapters 6); functions of representatives of workers on OSH; appointment, power, independence and functions of labour and OSH inspectors; composition and functions of workplace OSH committees; provisions on ergonomic constraints (Title V, chapters 7); individual and collective protective equipment (Title V, chapters 8);  workplace consultations on OSH, etc The ILO conducted consultations with Burundi&#x2019;s government and social partners since October 2019, until the launch of Burundi DWCP in Feb 2020 and technical advice during the development of the Labour Code and Decent Work Country Programme (DWCP) 2020-2023
With support from RBSA, the ILO mobilized technical support for the design and plan of the elaboration of a national OSH profile; it produced a gender sensitive situational analysis for the National OSH Profile and OSH Indicators.

The ILO facilitated the assembling of OSH experts including social partners for drafting of Guidelines  and provision of technical inputs.

The ILO sensitized tripartite stakeholders on OSH, including through a series of OSH and COVID19 trainings between April and August 2021, as well as produced and disseminated a series of Guidelines on COVID19  and OSH in collaboration with African Union Development Agency &#x2013;New Partnership for Africa&#x2019;s Development (AUDA-NEPAD) in 2020.

The ILO also did regular dissemination of new tools and up-to-date information by email to national OSH focal persons und Labour Ministries.</narrative>
   </description>
